1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,040
There's a version of you that most people will never meet, the one who replays a conversation

2
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:10,400
from three years ago at two in the morning, the one who smiled through a family dinner

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,200
while something inside quietly came apart.

4
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:19,280
The one who said, I'm fine so many times, it stopped being a centen, and started being

5
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:21,720
a place that you live.

6
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:27,040
We talk a lot about burnout, overwork, too many responsibilities, but there is another

7
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:31,120
kind of exhaustion, and it doesn't come from doing too much.

8
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:37,040
It comes from holding too much, it never shows up on a productivity tracker or a performance

9
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:38,040
review.

10
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:43,160
Nobody can see it, that's why I want to sit with this today, that weight and what it's

11
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:46,320
actually costing you to carry it.

12
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:50,360
You should know that you are measuring the wrong things.

13
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:55,480
Here is something that I have noticed in ten years of working in data.

14
00:00:55,480 --> 00:01:02,040
We are extremely good at measuring what's visible, output, revenue, churn, sleep hours,

15
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:03,040
steps.

16
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:08,440
We have built entire industries around quantifying being human, but we have almost no

17
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:15,600
language for what happens in the space between people, the grief with no name, the disappointment

18
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:21,360
you swallowed, the version of an event that nobody else witnessed, there is no metric

19
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:27,840
for the energy it takes to keep a composed face while something inside you is breaking

20
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:28,840
apart.

21
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:34,960
The American Psychological Association has strict stress in US for nearly two decades,

22
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:37,400
and the findings repeat every year.

23
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:44,480
We are more stressed than we report consistently that gap between what we feel and what we say

24
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:47,680
we feel isn't random, it's structured.

25
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:53,160
Something invisible things is one of the most normalized behaviors we have, and almost

26
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:56,400
nobody asks what it's doing to us over time.

27
00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:01,120
Then it leads you to the text that you are paying without a receipt.

28
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:07,000
There is a concept in psychology called cognitive load, the total mental resources you are using

29
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:08,520
at any moment.

30
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:13,720
Most people think of it as tasks, writing a report, managing a project, but your brain

31
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:18,320
doesn't separate a work problem from an emotional one.

32
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:24,400
It's all drawing from the same finite pool, so when you are carrying something unresolved,

33
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:30,000
an argument that ended without closure, a loss you never grieved, a resentment you have

34
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,320
held for years, your brain keeps returning to it, not because you are weak, because of

35
00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:41,680
something called the zeitgarnic effect, the mind holds onto incomplete things.

36
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:45,240
That's the same reason you remember the show you did not finish, better than the one

37
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:46,760
you did.

38
00:02:46,760 --> 00:02:51,800
The difference is emotional loops don't close with a click, they run in the background,

39
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:57,240
below awareness, eating the processing power, you were planning to use somewhere else.

40
00:02:57,240 --> 00:03:02,720
A few years ago, there was a stretch where I was publishing articles, recording videos,

41
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:07,160
building projects, and showing up to client meetings exactly as expected, from the outside

42
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:08,640
nothing looked wrong.

43
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:13,040
The work was still getting done, the deadlines were still being met.

44
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:19,280
What nobody could see was how much mental bandwidth was being consumed by a relationship that

45
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:22,040
was unraveling in a slow motion.

46
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:26,600
I would sit down to write and find myself rereading the same paragraph three times.

47
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:31,600
I would open my laptop with a clear plan and somehow end up staring at the screen, lost

48
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:34,440
in conversations that had happened days earlier.

49
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:40,240
The strange part was that I was still functioning well enough that nobody questioned it.

50
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:45,840
The visible version of me looked productive, the internal version was exhausted from carrying

51
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:52,680
an argument that never really ended in the future that suddenly felt uncertain.

52
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:56,960
Looking back, I wasn't struggling because I lacked discipline, I was paying attention

53
00:03:56,960 --> 00:04:02,680
to two realities at once, the work in front of me, and the emotional weight that I was

54
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:05,120
pretending wasn't there.

55
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:10,320
That's why on the day something is quietly wrong, you feel foggy, you forget small things,

56
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:15,800
40 minutes past at your desk with nothing to show, you're not lazy, you're running something

57
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:21,040
heavy in the background, and the research is consistent, suppressing an emotion doesn't

58
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:28,120
delete it, it relocates it into the body, into attention lapses, into a low grade irritability

59
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:32,120
everyone around you can feel and nobody can name.

60
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:38,000
It doesn't stay invisible, it leaks, here's the harder part to admit, the things you carry

61
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:44,440
in silence don't only land on you, they leak, usually not dramatically, in the half second

62
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:49,920
before you respond warmly in the patience that runs out a little faster than it should,

63
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:55,040
in the distance you keep from people trying to get close, not because you don't want

64
00:04:55,040 --> 00:05:01,880
connection, but because connection feels dangerous when you're already at capacity.

65
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:06,200
Someone carries something heavy, long enough, and they start to seem adjacent to their own

66
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:11,760
life, present, but not quite there, like there is a class between you.

67
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:16,800
Kids feel it first, they don't have words for, my parent is carrying and process grief,

68
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:19,080
but they know when someone is somewhere else.

69
00:05:19,080 --> 00:05:23,240
I remember a conversation with someone close to me who said, you're here, but you're

70
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:25,200
not really here anymore.

71
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:31,080
It wasn't said during a fight, it wasn't dramatic, in fact that's what made it hit so hard,

72
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:37,240
up until that moment I thought I was doing a good job containing everything, I thought

73
00:05:37,240 --> 00:05:43,880
carrying it silently was protecting the relationship from additional stress, what I did not realize

74
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:50,040
was that silence has a texture people can feel, the distance I believed was invisible had

75
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:54,720
already become visible to someone who cared about me.

76
00:05:54,720 --> 00:06:00,480
That conversation forced me to confront something really uncomfortable, the energy required

77
00:06:00,480 --> 00:06:05,720
to keep pretending I was okay, had become something greater, then the energy required

78
00:06:05,720 --> 00:06:08,520
to admit that I wasn't.

79
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:13,920
The people who love you can handle more than you think, what they cannot handle is the

80
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:20,480
static, the unnamed things between you that neither of you has language for.

81
00:06:20,480 --> 00:06:22,720
Why we don't put it down?

82
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:29,600
The common assumption is fear, fear of judgment of being a burden, sometimes that's true,

83
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:34,520
but underneath there is something else, putting something down requires you to look at it

84
00:06:34,520 --> 00:06:40,440
first, and when you have carried a loss for months or years it becomes part of the landscape,

85
00:06:40,440 --> 00:06:45,360
familiar, even the way it is familiar, the idea of feeling it instead of carrying it is

86
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:51,840
terrifying in a specific way, what if it is worse than I thought, once I look I cannot

87
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:58,320
function, so not looking becomes productive, at first when it calcifies, the thing that

88
00:06:58,320 --> 00:07:02,800
kept you functional becomes a thing, making you less functional, and some things don't

89
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:08,120
have clean endings anyway, the person isn't coming back, the apology isn't coming,

90
00:07:08,120 --> 00:07:12,120
carrying at least means you still have it, putting it down can feel like letting the

91
00:07:12,120 --> 00:07:18,880
loss become final, the work is quieter than you think, I'm not going to hand you a

92
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:24,320
five step framework here, if it were that simple you would have done it, the real work

93
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:30,280
is dramatic, it looks like telling one person one true thing about how you are, it looks

94
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:36,040
like admitting to yourself in plain language what happened and how it felt, there is a

95
00:07:36,040 --> 00:07:42,800
term in trauma research, titration, approaching something painful in small doses like diluting

96
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:47,800
a solution, you don't have to process it all at once, you don't have to say it publicly

97
00:07:47,800 --> 00:07:53,640
or ever, you just have to stop pretending it's not there, because something shifts when

98
00:07:53,640 --> 00:07:59,720
you name a thing accurately, even alone, the background process quits, not because it's

99
00:07:59,720 --> 00:08:06,880
resolved, but because the zi-carnic loop doesn't need resolution, it needs attention, the

100
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:12,560
cost of carrying things nobody sees is real, and it compounds, the longer you avoid looking,

101
00:08:12,560 --> 00:08:17,920
the heavier it gets, not because the thing grew, but because the effort of not looking

102
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:23,760
did, you're not as good at hiding it as you think, and even if you were, you deserve

103
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:31,440
to put it down, here is what I keep coming back to, we have made a virtue out of containment,

104
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:38,440
out of handling it, needing nothing, showing nothing, moving forward regardless, and sometimes

105
00:08:38,440 --> 00:08:45,120
that discipline is necessary, even beautiful, but somewhere along the way a lot of us stop

106
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:52,080
treating it as a temporary skill, and started treating it as an identity, I'm someone who

107
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:58,600
handles things, then the things accumulated, and the handling became the whole self, the

108
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:05,960
invisible load is real, the toll is real, and the first move, not the last, and only the

109
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:11,200
first is to stop calling it nothing, you don't have to share it, you don't have to resolve

110
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:18,200
it today, you just have to let it be what it is, something you are carrying that has

111
00:09:18,200 --> 00:09:26,200
weight, that deserves to be seen at minimum by you, this is breath of life, if something

112
00:09:26,200 --> 00:09:32,480
here landed, send it to one person who needs it, not as a conversation starter, just quietly,

113
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:37,360
the way you would leave a book on someone's desk, the people carrying invisible things

114
00:09:37,360 --> 00:09:44,360
rarely said out loud, but sometimes they notice when you notice.

